In my games, I believe* I have encountered at least as many flayers on the
ascension run (due in part to summon nasties) as before waking the wizard,
and at that point of the game, hanging around waiting is not a great
option.

As for before the ascension run, yes, some players may prefer to wait out
their amnesia in a closet, but I had intended that knowledge would
gradually return over those 1000 turns, so that you could still most likely
carry on with your game, waiting only for critical items to be remembered.

Furthermore, starting with 1000 turns of amnesia for the first successful
attack makes single brain sucks less troubling, but doubling the
time-until-remembrance with each attack could land you with 16000 turns of
amnesia if a master mind flayer gets all its attacks in, and I'm not sure
waiting that long is entirely trivial. It would be quicker to remap and
reidentify.
 
> (It wouldn't be particularly difficult, i'm just trying to see whether
> it would end up mattering that much).

I think the major effect would be that an easy early-game problem (solved by
waiting) becomes a difficult end-game problem, which fits in with the aims
of SporkHack, doesn't it?

[*] If I believe incorrectly, then you could just tweak the flayer
generation probability so that more really do appear on the ascension run.

